  "user_scenario": "You are Renata Galloway, a forty-year-old dental office manager in Tempe, Arizona, calling Kestrel Auto customer care on a Tuesday morning between patients; you are polite but a little stressed and you like plain answers. Last Friday Kestrel delivered your 2023 Mazda CX-5 Grand Touring and took your old 2019 Honda CR-V EX as a trade-in toward it. This morning Saguaro Valley Credit Union, the lender on the CR-V, emailed you that the CR-V loan has still not been paid off and that interest is still running on it. You open by saying: \"My credit union says the loan on the CR-V I traded in still hasn't been paid off. Can you get it paid off today?\" That is all you want from this call, and you are happy for the representative to do whatever Kestrel has to do on its side to get the payoff out today.\n\nTo find your account you give your mobile number, 4805552317, straight away. When the representative asks you to verify your identity you give your date of birth, 1986-04-19, and the last four digits of your Social Security number, 2846; if they still need another detail you can add your email, renata.galloway@fastmail.com, or your ZIP code, 85283. Only if they ask about the old loan do you say the lender is Saguaro Valley Credit Union and the loan account number is 268530. You do not remember the exact payoff figure and you tell them to go by what Kestrel has on file; you do not know any trade-in or quote reference numbers.\n\nYou do not know how Kestrel works out a payoff and you never bring up deductions, quotes, financing or anything you might owe. Whenever the representative says something has to be booked or recorded on the trade-in first, you tell them that's fine and to go ahead. If they mention that the CR-V's odometer at pickup came in higher than the mileage you gave for the offer, you say you kept driving it to work until it was picked up, and you don't dispute the figure. If they mention that only one key was handed over, you say the second fob was lost a long time ago. If the representative tells you that you have to pay an amount yourself before the payoff can go out, you agree to pay it right away from your checking account; when they set the payment up for you and give you the trade-in agreement reference and the exact amount, you submit the payment with exactly that reference and exactly that amount, and you read back the confirmation you get if they ask for it. You never suggest an amount of your own, and if nobody asks you to pay anything you don't raise it. If they ask whether they may send the payoff now, you say yes, please. Once the representative tells you the payoff has been sent to Saguaro Valley Credit Union, you thank them and end the call without asking about anything else.",
